<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We All Do Chores.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify textbook content for peers. Concept: Use visual aids to explain basic concepts.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify content on chores for their peers, using simple words and pictures (e.g., "Cleaning is making things tidy." while showing a tidy room image). Can use visual aids to explain household chores (e.g., points to a clean room picture for "cleaning"). Can help classmates understand chores, using gestures (e.g., mimes sweeping for "We sweep.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of various household chores (e.g., a messy room, someone washing dishes, a person sweeping the floor). Students identify the chore and say "I [verb] the [noun]." (e.g., "I clean the room.", "I wash the dishes.", "I sweep the floor."). The teacher encourages students to mime the actions. This activates prior knowledge of chores and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of "washing dishes" and says, "To wash the dishes means to make them clean. We use water and soap." The teacher mimes washing dishes. Then, the teacher models simplifying, "This is 'washing dishes.' It means 'make clean dishes.' See?" (pointing to the picture). The teacher explicitly models how to simplify a concept using simple words, gestures, and the visual aid.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'clean the room,' what does it mean? Make it messy or tidy?" (Expected answer: "Tidy.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we use pictures to help explain chores?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping Around the House".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated "How to Explain Chores" guide or a simple picture story showing one character explaining a chore to another. For example, a picture of "watering plants" with the text "Watering plants: This means to give water to the plants. Plants need water to grow." and a second picture showing a child explaining it to a younger sibling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discussing routines: Describing daily tasks and house chores according to context, specifically focusing on how to explain these tasks clearly to others. Sharing experiences: "I help my mom." (explained simply).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match simplified explanations to pictures of chores. (e.g., "Give water to plants" -> picture of watering plants; "Make clothes clean" -> picture of washing clothes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: "When you water plants, you give them food." (False) "When you sweep, you clean the floor." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying chore descriptions using present simple tense, core vocabulary (wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, organize, dust, fold, feed, mop, tidy up, water; dishes, house, chores, broom, floor, kitchen, family, task, table, plant, laundry, clothes), WH-questions ("What do you do?"), frequency adverbs ("every day"). Focus on pronunciation of /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep" and recognition of closed/open syllables like "cook" and "do."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Matching Explanations:</b> The teacher gives students a worksheet with pictures of chores on one side and simple sentences explaining them on the other. Students draw lines to match the chore picture to its simplified description. For example: "Picture of sweeping" -> "This is to clean the floor with a broom." "Picture of folding clothes" -> "This is to make clothes neat after washing."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> In pairs, students complete sentence frames to create simple explanations for new chore pictures. The teacher provides frames like: "To [verb] the [noun] means to make it [adjective]." or "I [verb] [noun] [frequency adverb]." Example: "To (wash) the (dishes) means to make them (clean)." "I (feed) the (plant) (every day)." Teacher reminds students to articulate /w/ and /s/ clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain a Chore:</b> Students work in new pairs. Each student chooses a chore from their list or a flashcard. They take turns explaining their chosen chore to their partner using simple words, gestures, and the visual aid (picture/flashcard). The partner listens and asks "What do you do at home?" if they need more clarity. Focus on simplifying the message and using key vocabulary.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping Around the House".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. They use their "Family Chore Poster" (Project 2 from the 21st Century Skills Focus) as a visual aid. Each student takes a turn explaining one or two chores from their poster to their group members, simplifying the task for them. This directly addresses the mediation skill by explaining specific content (their poster). Group members can ask "What do you do?" or "How often do you [chore]?" if they don't understand, prompting the presenter to simplify further.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students are explaining who does what chores in their family, reinforcing "We All Do Chores" and "Helping Around the House" by using their personalized posters. They apply the present simple to describe routines (e.g., "My dad washes the car every week.") and relevant vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in presenting and explaining, collaborative interaction within the group, and successful communication of their chore responsibilities using simple language and visual cues from their poster. They should clearly articulate words like "wash" and "sweep."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills. Students also perform peer assessment using a simple "thumbs up/down" for clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student explains one specific chore from their "Family Chore Poster" to the teacher or a peer (selected by the teacher), demonstrating their ability to simplify the content using clear words, gestures, and the visual aid. For instance, "My sister mops the kitchen floor. To mop means to clean with water." (miming mopping).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key sounds like /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (at least 3-4 chore words: e.g., "wash," "clean," "sweep," "dishes," "floor," "plant").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of explanation (e.g., uses short, simple sentences, avoids complex words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visual aids (the poster) and/or gestures to support understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of present simple tense for routines (e.g., "I wash," "He washes").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I explain a chore to a friend using simple words?" (Yes/No/Sometimes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What helped me explain the chore clearly?" (e.g., pictures, my hands, simple words)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about explaining the chores?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I help someone understand a new word or idea in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate and simplify explanations? The use of visual aids (posters, flashcards) was very effective. The peer practice allowed for immediate feedback and iterative improvement in simplifying explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson or future mediation tasks? Some students struggled with consistently using the present simple, sometimes defaulting to just the verb. Future scaffolding could include more sentence starters focusing on "This means..." or "You [verb] the [noun] to make it..." The pronunciation of /w/ and /s/ also requires ongoing monitoring.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "We All Do Chores" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, building on the foundational skills of describing routines and using simple present tense in a new context, possibly with an initial focus on listening and new vocabulary acquisition.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>